repository-code: 'https://github.com/suyogautam/Global-UHI-Analyzer-'
abstract: >-
  Urban heat islands (UHIs) — where city surfaces are
  significantly warmer than surrounding rural areas — are a
  well-documented consequence of urbanization, yet
  accessible tools for systematic long-term UHI monitoring
  remain limited. This open-source, web-based surface urban
  heat island (SUHI) analysis platform is built on Google
  Earth Engine and Streamlit, enabling satellite-derived
  thermal and land surface analysis for any user-defined
  area of interest without requiring local computing
  resources or programming expertise.

  The platform integrates two complementary satellite
  pipelines: Landsat Collection 2 Level-2 (Landsat 5/7/8/9,
  30 m spatial resolution, 2000–present) for fine-scale US
  analysis, and MODIS Terra/Aqua products (MOD11A1/MYD11A1,
  ~1 km, 2000–present; Terra + Aqua, Terra only or Aqua only)
  for global coverage including nighttime land surface
  temperature. For each year in a user-defined period, the
  app uses either the hottest month or the seasonal mean of
  the selected months, applies quality-controlled
  compositing, and derives land surface temperature (LST),
  NDVI, NDMI, and NDBI from atmospherically corrected surface
  reflectance. Urban and vegetated reference zones are
  delineated using either the National Land Cover Database
  (NLCD, 30 m, US-only) or MODIS MCD12Q1 IGBP land cover
  (500 m, global), with user-adjustable classes; by default
  cropland and water are excluded from the vegetated
  reference. SUHI intensity is the LST difference between
  urban and vegetated-reference pixels. Temporal trends are
  assessed with Sen's slope and the Mann–Kendall test
  (original or Hamed–Rao modified) on the unsmoothed annual
  series; a 3-year moving average is shown for display only.
  Each year reports valid-pixel counts, and the app flags
  years with a very small vegetated reference or no valid
  data. Satellite LST anomalies are compared with ERA5-Land
  2 m air temperature anomalies as a consistency check.
  Outputs include interactive multi-layer maps, downloadable
  results tables, trend charts, GeoTIFF rasters, and
  shapefiles with per-year attributes. Core calculations are
  covered by automated unit tests.

  The tool is designed for researchers, urban planners, and
  climate practitioners seeking reproducible, multi-decadal
  SUHI assessments across diverse geographic contexts and
  spatial scales.
